  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam has been working with local partners to address the extreme water insecurity faced by populations in Kenya&#x2019;s Arid and Semi-Arid lands.  At baseline, 65% of the project&#x2019;s target participants did not have access to water for domestic consumption and were trekking up to 40kms to search for water for domestic and livestock use.  In collaboration with the Government of Marsabit, Oxfam and implementing partner Strategies for Northen Development (SND) equipped one of the deepest boreholes in the country with a solarised pumping system.  As a result, 4,000 households in Kubi Dibaya, Saku Ward of Marsabit County now have access to water, with 83.1% of the targeted households reporting safe access to at least 15 litres per person per day.  Water access was improved for an additional 1,800 households in Turkana County after the rehabilitation of the Kangakipur borehole. Rehabilitation works included the construction of a 100M3 elevated steel tank, pipeline extension and construction of water points.  The borehole is currently serving 9,100 people (3,841 men and 5,259 women, 27 PLWD including 15 men and 12 women). To ensure sustainability and continued and effective operations, community-based committees have also been established and trained to manage the water projects.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP) response led by Plan International delivered food security and livelihoods support, multi-purpose cash assistance and nutrition services in three regions of Ethiopia. In Oromia, the project addressed child malnutrition caused by the drought through the strengthening of local service systems to health institutions. This included providing technical and logistical support to 34 health facilities and 99 health workers, supplying logistical and transportation services for medical supplies to remote areas, and setting up parent-parent support groups together with training and skills. Nutritional screening identified 982 children (497 boys, 485 girls) as Severe Acute Malnutrition cases. 16,201 children (12,018 females and 4,183 males) and 7,874 pregnant and lactating women were also identified as cases of Moderate Acute Malnutrition. As a result, 110 children (53 females and 57 males) were admitted to the stabilization centre and 872 children (441 females and 431 males) were admitted to the outpatient programs of the health facilities and treated with therapeutic feeding. Other cases were linked to services including health, mental health and psychosocial support, protection, nutrition assistance, multipurpose cash transfers and livelihoods support.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The protection response in Ethiopia aimed to strengthen the protection system and improve access to services for women and children affected by humanitarian crisis in nine target woredas in Amhara, Tigray and Oromia. AHP partners provided gender-based violence case management and referral pathways, service delivery training, psychosocial support services, multipurpose cash support and dignity kits to 2,939 participants, including 2,770 women and girls. Seven Child-Friendly Spaces and eight Women and Girls Safe Spaces were set up to provide protection services and empower participants with positive coping skills. Awareness campaigns on key protection topics reached 21,859 participants to enhance knowledge on gender-based violence and its prevention.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under the Food Security and Livelihoods Response in Kenya, Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP) partners in four counties reviewed market systems and conducted value chain analysis to inform livelihood options following droughts and floods. In Marsabit, the market assessment informed interventions such as livestock restocking, farmer training, and cash grants, with an emphasis on empowering women and people with disabilities. As a result, 600 shoats (sheep and goats) were distributed to 200 households (64 male-headed and 136 female-headed. Similar assessments in Garissa and Tana River revealed gender disparities in value chain participation, leading to targeted support for farmer groups and cooperative development. In Turkana, Village Savings and Loan Associations, fisherfolk, and livestock groups were strengthened through grants and climate-smart agriculture initiatives, including the establishment of kitchen gardens to boost nutrition.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With support through the Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP), Oxfam conducted a multi-sectoral emergency response in conflict and drought-affected areas of Tigray, Afar and Amhara Regions of Ethiopia. Food security and livelihood activities included the provision of early maturing drought resilient crop seeds and farming tools, livestock restocking, the development of irrigation schemes, and capacity strengthening of Savings and Credit Cooperatives. Overall, the project benefitted 13,745 individuals including 7,482 women, 1,662 children under 5 years old and 270 persons with disabilities. The end-line results showed a 60% decrease in the use of negative coping strategies among targeted households, reflecting a marked improvement in their livelihoods and resilience to future shocks.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
